## Why This Is Asked

This question tests resilience and reliability fundamentals using real-world API interaction patterns. It evaluates the ability to design concrete tests, leverage mocks effectively, and reason about backoff strategies, idempotency, and rate limits—core skills for building dependable services.

## Key Concepts

- API mocking with deterministic fixtures
- Retry and backoff strategies with configurable caps
- Idempotent retry semantics to ensure safety
- Per-client rate limiting under concurrent scenarios
- CI integration for reproducible, automated testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example scaffold using a mocking library
const nock = require('nock');
const { pricingClient } = require('./pricingClient');

describe('Pricing API Client', () => {
  beforeEach(() => nock.cleanAll());

  test('retries on 429 with exponential backoff', async () => {
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .reply(429, 'Too Many Requests', { 'Retry-After': '1' })
      .get('/price')
      .reply(200, { price: 99.99 });

    const result = await pricingClient.getPrice();
    expect(result.price).toBe(99.99);
    expect(scope.isDone()).toBe(true);
  });

  test('retries on 500 with exponential backoff', async () => {
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .reply(500)
      .get('/price')
      .reply(200, { price: 49.99 });

    const result = await pricingClient.getPrice();
    expect(result.price).toBe(49.99);
  });

  test('respects max retry cap', async () => {
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .times(4)
      .reply(500);

    await expect(pricingClient.getPrice()).rejects.toThrow('Max retries exceeded');
  });

  test('idempotent retries do not cause side effects', async () => {
    let callCount = 0;
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .reply(() => {
        callCount++;
        return [500, {}];
      })
      .get('/price')
      .reply(200, { price: 100 });

    await pricingClient.getPrice();
    expect(callCount).toBe(1); // First call failed, second succeeded
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
Zero-downtime migrations across regions are high-stakes in real services. This question probes practical testing, validation across replicas, and rollback safety.

## Key Concepts
- Zero-downtime schema changes
- Shadow migrations and data drift checks
- Canary/Rolling updates with feature flags
- Observability: latency, error rates, RPO

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test scaffold for verification of row counts before/after migration
const {Client} = require('pg');
const a = new Client({/* src DB */});
const b = new Client({/* dst DB */});
async function verifyCounts(){
  await a.connect(); await b.connect();
  const [ra] = await a.query('SELECT count(*) FROM users');
  const [rb] = await b.query('SELECT count(*) FROM users');
  return ra.count === rb.count;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle drift detection if data types are incompatible across regions?
- What metrics define a successful cutover and when to rollback?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for offline-first behavior are core for reliability but beginners often struggle with storage APIs and network conditions. This question isolates those concepts in a tangible, front-end context.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first strategy
- IndexedDB usage
- Sync mechanics and idempotence
- Conflict resolution policy
- CI network condition emulation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Cypress skeleton for offline caching test
describe('Offline-first sync', () => {
  it('caches submissions offline and syncs when online', () => {
    cy.visit('/form');
    cy.window().then(w => { w.navigator.onLine = false; });
    cy.get('#name').type('Alice');
    cy.get('#email').type('a@example.com');
    cy.get('#submit').click();
    // verify IndexedDB contains 1 entry
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt tests for different storage backends (IndexedDB vs LocalStorage)?
